from build import put,render
put('fgr2r',title='Sub-Instruction Aware Vision-and-Language Navigation',authors=['Yicong Hong','Cristian Rodriguez-Opazo','Qi Wu','Stephen Gould'],year=2020,venue='EMNLP 2020',pages=['3–9','11–17'],problem='R2R endpoint success can conceal failure to execute intermediate instructions. The paper introduces human sub-instruction–subtrajectory alignment and a recurrent controller that explicitly tracks a single instruction chunk. The environment is the discretized Matterport3D graph; instructions and route segments are assumed to be monotonically ordered.',method='A Stanford dependency-parser heuristic splits instructions at roots, conjunctions, and parataxis, then merges short or action-only fragments (Algorithm 1). The full instruction is encoded by an LSTM before hidden states are partitioned, preserving earlier linguistic context. A chunk-specific attention replaces whole-instruction attention. An LSTM-based shifting head combines the current state, memory, selected-action visual feature, attended chunk, and one-hot count of remaining chunks (Eqs. 4–6). It can advance exactly one chunk and cannot return to an earlier chunk. Action cross-entropy and balanced binary shifting cross-entropy supervise training; student forcing supplies action rollouts while chunk shifts use teacher forcing. Ground-truth shift is positive within 0.5 m of the annotated segment endpoint. The Back-Translation variant applies shifting loss only to its imitation branch. Inference uses predicted shifts and actions. FGR2R adds an average of 3.6 chunks per instruction and 2.4 viewpoints per chunk. Fifty-eight qualified annotators survived a 126-person qualification process. Single-viewpoint chunks are merged during training but retained in validation (Appendix A.1). Models use the original baseline hyperparameters and one Tesla K80.',novelty='Relative to soft whole-instruction attention and progress monitors, the contribution is explicit chunk selection, supervised transitions, and traceable intermediate evaluation. Concurrent BabyWalk uses automatically aligned chunks and curriculum learning, whereas FGR2R uses human alignment. The method is not an instruction-error detector or backward recovery controller.',results='PAPER EXPERIMENT: Table 1, PDF p. 7, reports unseen Speaker-Follower SR 29.8→34.7 and nDTW 0.54→0.59; Self-Monitoring SR 40.7→42.4. Back-Translation barely changes, SR 46.6→46.7, while nDTW falls 0.70→0.69. Table 2 shows best shifting F1 only 0.331 and recall 0.209 despite precision 0.806. Thus explicit alignment helps some agents, but correct timing remains weak. Table 3 finds greater endpoint errors for object-specific or condition-dependent chunks; this is descriptive clustering, not a causal isolation of language difficulty. Appendix Table 4 shows R4R Back-Translation SR 31.2→21.5 despite nDTW 0.39→0.48: concatenation leaves bridge viewpoints without instructions and introduces intermediate STOP language. Figure 10 provides a negative case in which perfect chunk shifting still fails object grounding. These results separate progress tracking, grounding, and endpoint success.',implementation='Official repository located and pinned; source audit continues. No repository code has been executed.',bottlenecks=[dict(id='fgr2r-transition',description='Chunk completion has low recall and cannot reverse an erroneous advance.',conditions='Agent deviates from its annotated subpath or must revisit an earlier instruction after backtracking.',cause='Training supplies teacher chunk transitions, whereas inference predicts them; the state machine allows only one forward transition.',evidence='PAPER EXPERIMENT: Table 2 best F1 0.331; AUTHOR ACKNOWLEDGEMENT: Appendix A.1 leaves backward/multi-step shifts to future work.',kind='Methodological',followups=['BabyWalk','Landmark-RxR','GELA','VLN-Trans','M-TRACK'],status='Partially addressed',confidence='High within the reported discretized R2R protocol')],findings=[dict(claim='Correct chunk shifting alone does not establish landmark grounding.',evidence_type='PAPER EXPERIMENT',source='Supplied PDF',locator='Appendix Figure 10, PDF p. 17')],sources=[dict(url='https://aclanthology.org/2020.emnlp-main.271/',locator='Official proceedings metadata and paper'),dict(url='https://github.com/YicongHong/Fine-Grained-R2R',locator='Author-linked release')],associated=['Appendix A and qualitative examples embedded in supplied PDF, pp. 11–17'],limits=['Code inspection remains in progress; no benchmark executed.'])
put('uln',title='ULN: Towards Underspecified Vision-and-Language Navigation',authors=['Weixi Feng','Tsu-Jui Fu','Yujie Lu','William Yang Wang'],year=2022,venue='EMNLP 2022',pages=['3–9','13–19'],problem='An agent trained on detailed route instructions often fails when users omit details or provide only a destination. R2R-ULN progressively simplifies existing instructions into L1 (redundancy removed), L2 (partial route), and L3 (goal description). It tests English indoor graph navigation, not arbitrary contradictory instructions.',method='A pooled-word classifier selects low-level versus high-level instruction processing. Granularity Specific Sub-networks (GSS) are identified by transplanting components from a separately trained goal-only agent and measuring validation gain: the recurrent VLN-BERT text encoder or HAMT cross-modal encoder is the selected component. That component is retrained on R2R-last with the remaining agent frozen. At inference an uncertainty MLP consumes visual-to-text attention and action logits. It is trained from randomly dropped training sub-instructions, with teacher-action disagreement as the binary label. Above threshold 0.5, the agent physically explores top candidates and scores current plus future maximum logits (Eq. 7); history state is frozen to delay premature text progression. Reported settings use one-step lookahead, gamma 1.2, and at most three exploration triggers. Training uses original R2R and last sentences, mixed imitation/A2C, AdamW on one GPU: base/classifier 300,000 iterations, selected high-level component 10,000 iterations, uncertainty model 10 epochs. No ULN evaluation instructions are required as training examples, but the architecture-selection protocol measures L3 validation performance and should be separated from held-out testing.',novelty='Combines granularity-specific routing with a learned grounding-error trigger and explicit lookahead. Active VLN instead trains a separate exploration policy using navigation reward; ULN reuses its policy and freezes history. It is prior art for uncertainty-triggered navigation exploration and text-conditioned expert routing, not a zero-training method.',results='PAPER EXPERIMENT: Table 4, PDF p. 7, recurrent baseline unseen SR/SPL 39.3/35.0; GSS 42.3/37.7; GSS plus E2E 44.7/29.7, with path length 12.95→23.02 m relative to GSS. HAMT GSS 43.5/39.5 becomes 44.6/25.9 with E2E and path length 12.91→28.31 m. Table 7 makes the tradeoff explicit: threshold zero can collapse SPL although SR rises. Table 5 does not uniformly support improvement at every level: HAMT L1 SR 56.4→55.5 with full method. Human L3 SR is 75 versus L0 86 (Table 2), so underspecification can also increase human ambiguity. Appendix Table 12 finds adding REVERIE/SOON is not automatically useful. Default evaluation subsamples levels at unequal rates; full-set ablations must not be mixed with Table 4 denominators. The paper establishes useful SR gains, not efficient or calibrated uncertainty resolution.',implementation='Official repository pinned; README explicitly lists incomplete release tasks. Source inspection is pending and should determine whether HAMT, training scripts, and checkpoints are actually present. No code executed.',bottlenecks=[dict(id='uln-exploration-cost',description='Uncertainty-triggered exploration increases success while sharply reducing path efficiency.',conditions='Underspecified instructions, repeated uncertainty triggers, and physical lookahead/backtracking.',cause='The trigger predicts disagreement with a teacher action rather than calibrated value of information; future-logit scores are heuristic and exploration is not free.',evidence='PAPER EXPERIMENT: Tables 4 and 7, Appendix Table 13; AUTHOR ACKNOWLEDGEMENT: §5.2.3 uncertainty is least accurate near the early corrections where intervention matters.',kind='Methodological',followups=['Meta-Explore','Explore until Confident','KnowNo','AIUTA'],status='Shifted into another cost or limitation',confidence='High for the measured SR–SPL tradeoff; no universal impossibility claim')],findings=[dict(claim='One-step lookahead is paid physical exploration, and improved SR does not imply improved SPL.',evidence_type='PAPER EXPERIMENT',source='Supplied PDF',locator='Tables 4 and 7, pp. 7–8')],sources=[dict(url='https://aclanthology.org/2022.emnlp-main.429/',locator='Official proceedings'),dict(url='https://github.com/weixi-feng/ULN',locator='Author-linked implementation')],associated=['Appendices A–B embedded in main PDF, pp. 13–19'],limits=['Figures were read through labels and captions; no independent trajectory relabelling. Code audit ongoing.'])
put('babywalk',title='BabyWalk: Going Farther in Vision-and-Language Navigation by Taking Baby Steps',authors=['Wang Zhu','Hexiang Hu','Jiacheng Chen','Zhiwei Deng','Vihan Jain','Eugene Ie','Fei Sha'],year=2020,venue='ACL 2020',pages=['3–8','12–14'],problem='Policies learned on short route instructions lose fidelity on longer routes. BabyWalk studies transfer across R2R, R4R, and constructed R6R/R8R sequences in the same Matterport3D navigation formalism. Longer tasks are concatenations of R2R routes, not independently authored real-world missions.',method='Heuristics extract landmark phrases and chunk instructions into baby steps. A weakly supervised landmark classifier uses trajectory-level object-word labels: state logits are max-pooled and optimized with binary cross-entropy. Dynamic programming chooses monotonically ordered chunk endpoints from average landmark scores (Appendix A). Each chunk has a language LSTM; past chunks and their executed trajectories are separately encoded, exponentially weighted with recency coefficient 0.5, and fused by an MLP. A recurrent cross-modal policy consumes current panorama, prior action, current chunk and remembered context. Imitation learning initially resets the agent to expert chunk-start context; curriculum policy-gradient training then executes increasingly long suffixes of one to four chunks. Terminal reward is SR plus CLS, with zero intermediate reward (Appendix B.2). Inference needs only heuristic chunks and the agent’s own trajectories, not expert alignment. Features are ResNet-152 2048 plus 128-dimensional tiled orientation; hidden size 512, GloVe words 300. Adam learning rate 1e-4, 50,000 imitation iterations then 10,000 per lecture; RL samples eight trajectories per instruction and discounts by 0.95.',novelty='The combination of weak automatic chunk alignment, hierarchical recency memory, and length curriculum distinguishes BabyWalk from Speaker-Follower, RCM, and progress-monitor agents. Compared with concurrent FGR2R, supervision is automatically aligned rather than human chunk annotations. Memory forgetting and curriculum should be credited separately from additional speaker augmentation.',results='PAPER EXPERIMENT: Table 2, PDF p. 7, R4R-trained BabyWalk achieves R4R SR 29.6, CLS 47.8, SDTW 18.1 versus FAST 36.2/34.0/15.5. It prioritizes route fidelity rather than winning endpoint SR. R8R SDTW 11.5 exceeds FAST 6.3, but R8R SR remains 26.3. Table 3, p. 8, removing memory lowers R4R SDTW 17.3→9.9; learned LSTM summary yields 14.4. Table 4 separates curriculum from ordinary RL: SDTW 13.6→17.3 in-domain and 14.1→17.5 on other datasets. Speaker-augmented BabyWalk has lower SR than its unaugmented counterpart in several settings; extra data do not uniformly improve the target metric. The evidence supports hierarchical training for concatenated length transfer, not arbitrary compositional generalization.',implementation='Official repository pinned and source files downloaded. Actual model/training/preprocessing/evaluation source audit remains in progress; no code executed.',bottlenecks=[dict(id='babywalk-composition',description='Long-route gains rely on heuristic chunk boundaries and weak landmark alignment, with low final success on the longest routes.',conditions='Repeated landmarks, object-poor clauses, incorrect chunk endpoints, or missions whose dependencies extend beyond recent chunks.',cause='Monotonic dynamic programming and exponential forgetting encode a particular compositional prior; training uses artificial concatenations and expert-start curriculum.',evidence='PAPER EXPERIMENT: R8R SR 26.3, Table 2; DEDUCTION from §§4.1–4.4 and Appendix A/B, not a measured repeated-landmark failure rate.',kind='Methodological and insufficiently tested',followups=['FGR2R','Landmark-RxR','HAMT','M-TRACK'],status='Partially addressed',confidence='High for reported low success; medium for inferred failure conditions')],findings=[dict(claim='Curriculum increases route fidelity beyond ordinary RL, while stronger endpoint SR is not established.',evidence_type='PAPER EXPERIMENT',source='Supplied PDF',locator='Tables 2 and 4, pp. 7–8')],sources=[dict(url='https://aclanthology.org/2020.acl-main.229/',locator='Official proceedings'),dict(url='https://github.com/Sha-Lab/babywalk',locator='Author implementation')],limits=['Remaining appendix pages and source audit still in progress.'])
